# Operator progress logs

When an agent produces something the user has to wait on — a capture, scrape, export, migration, transcription, batch transform, or other multi-item job — the terminal log must show what is happening while it runs. A bar stuck at zero, a frozen clock, or a line that says "write" when nothing was saved is not an acceptable log.

## Live line

Use [Rich](https://pypi.org/project/rich/) for jobs that stream child-process output. An in-process Python loop may use [tqdm](https://pypi.org/project/tqdm/) only when the bar shows the same fields below.

The live line must include:

1. A spinner that keeps moving during waits.
2. A short job name, plus the current item while that item is in progress.
3. A bar, a percentage, and a finished/total count. Finished includes saved items and deferred or failed items. A run that is only failing must not stay at 0.
4. A rate in items per minute, based on finished items.
5. Elapsed time that advances during a wait, not a timestamp frozen at the last event.
6. Remaining time or ETA.
7. Separate saved and deferred counts. Name them for the job if those words do not fit, but keep success and failure apart.

## Event lines

Print these above the live line, in color:

- Start of the current item.
- Success, including the identity of what was saved. Green.
- Deferred or failed item, including which item and why. Yellow. Keep going, and keep the good results already written.
- A status line with saved, deferred, and what is next. Cyan. Do not say "write" unless a record was actually saved.
- A fatal stop. Red.

## Skip

Skip the live display for a command that finishes in a few seconds, a unit test, or a one-line check.

